    description:
      "If the Commercial 1750 wins on features, the Sole F80 wins on build, and that's why it holds the runner-up spot in our rankings. The <strong>22 x 60 inch running deck</strong> is among the largest in this guide alongside the Bowflex, and that matters for taller users, since runners over about six feet often find 20-inch-wide, shorter decks cramped. The <strong>375 lb weight capacity</strong> is the highest figure in this guide, and a capacity that high generally signals a heavier, more rigid frame that moves less during hard interval efforts than lighter machines. Sole's cushioning system uses individual shock absorbers under the deck rather than a single foam layer, which is designed to reduce joint impact compared with firmer decks. The <strong>3.5 CHP motor</strong> is a solid continuous rating for mixed-pace training. Where the F80 shows its age is the console, a 9-inch LCD rather than a touchscreen, and the built-in speakers are a basic feature rather than a highlight. But Sole doesn't lock any core functionality behind a subscription, so what you buy is what you get, permanently. At <strong>$1,699 to $1,999</strong>, it's a smart choice for anyone who prioritizes long-term durability and legroom over flashy screens.",
  },
  {
    id: "horizon-78at",
    name: "Horizon Fitness 7.8 AT",
    editorialScore: 4.5,
    category: "Mid-to-Premium Home Cardio",
    badge: "Best Value Premium",
    amazonUrl:
