    pros: [
      "Massive 22-inch rotating touchscreen doubles as a strength-training display",
      "JRNY app adapts workouts automatically to your fitness level over time",
      "Roomy running surface accommodates long strides comfortably",
      "Side handrails support optional dumbbell and resistance-band work",
      "Sturdy frame with minimal deck flex even at higher speeds",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Among the most expensive machines in this entire roundup",
      "JRNY subscription is needed to unlock the adaptive coaching features",
      "Large footprint demands a genuinely dedicated corner of a room",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The best choice if a giant touchscreen and strength add-ons matter more to you than staying on a budget.",
    description:
      "The Bowflex Treadmill 22 is built around one standout feature, its <strong>22-inch rotating HD touchscreen</strong>, and it's clear why Bowflex made it the centerpiece. The screen swivels so you can rotate it toward a yoga mat or set of dumbbells and follow a JRNY-guided strength circuit right after your cardio session, something none of the other treadmills in this guide can really replicate. On the running side, the deck is among the roomiest in this roundup, giving taller runners extra length for a full stride at faster paces. The JRNY app is the real engine here: it tracks your performance over time and is designed to gradually adjust recommended paces and inclines as your fitness improves. The frame is built to match the price, and the side handrails are designed to support light dumbbell work rather than just decoration. The tradeoffs are cost and footprint. At <strong>$2,999 to $3,499</strong>, it's the priciest machine here, and JRNY's full adaptive features require an ongoing subscription. The frame is also large, so measure your space carefully before buying. For hybrid cardio-and-strength households, though, it's a genuinely unique option.",
