        <div id="setup-and-maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Treadmill Setup, Break-In, and Maintenance</h2>

        <h3>Where to Place It</h3>
        <p>
          Set your treadmill on a hard, level surface, hardwood, tile, or a dedicated exercise mat over carpet, never directly on thick carpet, which can trap heat around the motor and cause the machine to sit unevenly. Leave at least <strong>two feet of clearance</strong> on all sides and behind the machine, and make sure you have roughly <strong>8 feet of ceiling height</strong> if you plan to use taller users' full range of motion at higher inclines. Good airflow around the motor housing also helps prevent premature overheating. Basements and garages are popular choices, but keep an eye on humidity and temperature extremes in unfinished spaces, since both can accelerate wear on belts, electronics, and lubricant over time.
        </p>

        <h3>Assembly Time Expectations</h3>
        <p>
          Most home treadmills in this guide take <strong>60 to 120 minutes</strong> to assemble with two people, and we'd budget closer to the higher end for larger, feature-rich machines like the NordicTrack Commercial 1750 or Horizon 7.8 AT. Simpler machines like the Schwinn 810 typically assemble faster. Many retailers offer paid white-glove assembly if you'd rather skip the process entirely. Whichever route you choose, keep the original packaging and hardware bag until you've confirmed the machine runs correctly through a full range of speeds and inclines, since most manufacturers require the original box for warranty returns.
        </p>

        <h3>Belt Lubrication Schedule</h3>
        <p>
          Most belts need fresh silicone lubricant roughly every <strong>150-300 miles</strong>, though some newer decks come pre-lubricated with a longer interval; check your manual for the specific recommendation. Skipping lubrication accelerates belt and deck wear and is one of the fastest ways to shorten a treadmill's usable life. A quick way to check if lubrication is overdue is to run a hand under the belt's edge while the machine is unplugged; if it feels dry rather than lightly tacky, it's time to reapply.
        </p>

        <h3>Belt Alignment and Centering</h3>
        <p>
          Belts drift off-center over time, especially in the first few weeks of use as everything settles in. Most machines have two adjustment bolts at the rear roller; small quarter-turn adjustments, checked after a short test walk, are usually enough to recenter a drifting belt. Address it early, since a persistently off-center belt can wear unevenly and eventually need full replacement. If you notice the belt rubbing against one side of the frame or hear a new scraping sound during a walk, treat it as a signal to check alignment right away rather than waiting for a scheduled maintenance check.
        </p>

        <h3>Cleaning Routine</h3>
        <p>
          Wipe down the deck, rails, and console after each use to prevent sweat buildup, and vacuum underneath and around the motor housing every few weeks to keep dust and pet hair from clogging ventilation. A quick weekly wipe-down takes less than five minutes and meaningfully extends the life of the belt and electronics. Households with pets or carpeted rooms nearby should vacuum more frequently, since airborne fibers and hair are one of the most common causes of premature motor wear we've seen reported.
        </p>

        <div class="not-prose bg-red-50 border border-red-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
