            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 text-lg mb-2">What horsepower do I need for a home treadmill?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              For walking and light jogging, a <strong>2.5-3.0 CHP</strong> motor is enough. If you plan to run regularly or share the machine with multiple household members, look for at least <strong>3.5 CHP</strong>, and serious runners should target <strong>3.75 CHP or higher</strong> for reliable long-term performance. Remember to check the continuous duty rating specifically, not the peak horsepower figure printed in bold on the box, since peak numbers can overstate what a motor can actually sustain during a real workout.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 text-lg mb-2">Is an iFit or JRNY subscription worth the extra cost?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              If you're motivated by trainer-led workouts, scenic routes, and automatically adjusted incline and speed, subscriptions like iFit or JRNY genuinely improve the experience and are worth it for most people. If you prefer to set your own pace manually, you can skip them and still use a treadmill's core functions, though double-check what happens to the console and touchscreen features if you eventually let the subscription lapse.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h4 class="font-black text-gray-900 text-lg mb-2">How much weight capacity should I look for in a treadmill?</h4>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
              Choose a treadmill rated for at least <strong>50 pounds more</strong> than the heaviest user's body weight. Most home machines fall between 300 and 375 pounds, though some models like the <strong>Sole F80</strong> push closer to 375 pounds for extra headroom and durability. A higher-rated capacity is also a decent shortcut for judging overall frame and motor quality, even if you'll never approach the stated limit yourself.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h4 class="font-black text-gray-900 text-lg mb-2">Where should I place a treadmill in my house?</h4>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
              Choose a flat, hard-flooring area with at least <strong>two feet of clearance</strong> behind and to the sides of the machine, and roughly <strong>eight feet of ceiling height</strong> if you plan to use the incline feature. A dedicated treadmill mat helps protect flooring and reduce vibration and noise. Basements and spare rooms tend to work best, both for sound isolation and because a treadmill's weight and vibration are easier to manage on a ground-level, well-supported floor.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h4 class="font-black text-gray-900 text-lg mb-2">How long do home treadmills typically last?</h4>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
              With regular maintenance, most home treadmills last <strong>7 to 10 years</strong>. Budget models with lighter-duty motors tend toward the shorter end, while treadmills with 3.5 CHP or higher motors and heavier frames, like the Sole F80 or NordicTrack Commercial 1750, often last longer with proper belt lubrication and cleaning. Consistent upkeep, especially belt lubrication and keeping the motor compartment free of dust, is usually a bigger factor in lifespan than the specific brand you choose.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h4 class="font-black text-gray-900 text-lg mb-2">Do I need a treadmill mat?</h4>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
              Yes, in most cases. A treadmill mat protects flooring from vibration and debris, reduces noise transfer to rooms below, and helps keep the machine level and stable. It's a low-cost addition that's worth adding regardless of which treadmill you buy, and it also makes cleanup easier by catching dust and sweat that would otherwise land directly on carpet or hardwood.
            </p>
